## The story

It is {{RESULT:story.local_time}} at Mumbai. A narrowbody (fictional tail {{RESULT:story.tail}}) about to fly back to Abu Dhabi has its **{{RESULT:story.pn}}** {{RESULT:story.desc}} removed unserviceable. It is needed before the next departure (rectification window D0, hypothetical).

BOM holds no serviceable spare. The legacy rule holds {{RESULT:story.P0_station_units}} units of this part at BOM and {{RESULT:story.P0_outstation_units}} at all six outstations together, because it puts a line-station unit only where a D0 part has at least 4 quoted removals a year at that station, and BOM's quoted rate for this part is below that. AUH has {{RESULT:story.auh_on_hand}} on the shelf. The AOG desk ships one on the next flight out: {{RESULT:story.wait_h}} in transit, then {{RESULT:story.fitting_h}} of fitting and release to service. In the simulated history this exact incident is {{RESULT:story.incident_id}}: {{RESULT:story.aog_hours}} of material-related downtime. P2 holds {{RESULT:story.P2_station_units}} units at BOM and {{RESULT:story.P2_outstation_units}} across the outstations.

Nothing went "wrong" here. The removal rate, the repair turnaround and the positioning rule together made this grounding likely. That is the question this project answers at fleet scale: which parts, at which stations, carry the material shortage exposure, and what is the cheapest way to take it off the table.

The full worked example is in the [study guide](STUDY_GUIDE.md).

