## Six questions this answers

**1. Which parts drive material shortage exposure?**
Of 120 part numbers, {{RESULT:parts.top_risk.count}} are flagged urgent (at least 25 hours a year of exposure: legacy-history material AOG plus modelled residual downtime under the frontier option). Together they account for {{RESULT:parts.top_risk.share_of_aog_hours_pct}} of the legacy (P0) history's material AOG hours. The top part is {{RESULT:parts.top_risk.rank1.pn}}, at {{RESULT:parts.top_risk.rank1.risk_hours}} hours a year, with the largest share of its history downtime at {{RESULT:parts.top_risk.rank1.station}}.

**2. Which forecasting method wins, by demand class? Does the reliability model beat the Croston family?**
Champions were frozen per demand class × part class on the selection window and re-scored once on the holdout: best in {{RESULT:honest.champions_best}} cells. For removal-driven parts (holdout) the exposure-based reliability model scores mean MASE {{RESULT:forecast.reliability.mase}} against {{RESULT:forecast.best_croston_family.mase}} for the best Croston-family method ({{RESULT:forecast.best_croston_family.method}}), a paired difference of {{RESULT:forecast.reliability_vs_croston.delta_mase}} (95% CI {{RESULT:forecast.reliability_vs_croston.delta_mase_ci}}). Verdict: {{RESULT:forecast.reliability_vs_croston.verdict}}. Where it does win is the upper quantile that stocking depends on: {{RESULT:forecast.reliability.pinball_summary}}.

**3. What float do we recommend, versus the baseline?**
Total rotable spares (serviceable stock plus the {{RESULT:policy.loop_units}} units seeded in the repair loop, common to all plans): P0 {{RESULT:policy.P0.rotable_total_spares}}, P1 {{RESULT:policy.P1.rotable_total_spares}}, P2 {{RESULT:policy.P2.rotable_total_spares}}, I125 {{RESULT:policy.I125.rotable_total_spares}}.

**4. Hub or outstation?**
Both, in proportion. Under P0, {{RESULT:network.P0.outstation_share}} of material AOG is at the outstations ({{RESULT:network.P0.outstation_hours}} a year), because P0 holds only {{RESULT:network.P0.outstation_rotables}} rotables there. P1 sizes every station on its own demand and puts {{RESULT:network.P1.outstation_rotables}} rotables forward; P2 pools the float at AUH and positions {{RESULT:network.P2.outstation_rotables}}, only where a D0 miss costs more than the unit's carrying cost. Paired against P1, P2 changes material AOG by {{RESULT:network.P2_vs_P1.delta_aog_hours}} {{RESULT:network.P2_vs_P1.delta_aog_hours_ci}} a year with {{RESULT:network.P2_vs_P1.delta_capital_aed}}: starving the hub to stock outstations moves the shortage to AUH rather than removing it.

**5. What does the Readiness Frontier recommend?**
P2 is already near the frontier: at P2's capital the best re-optimised allocation is {{RESULT:frontier.B100_vs_P2}} vs P2 (not significant). The gain beyond P2 comes from more capital. The pre-stated break-even rule picks I125 on the selection seeds; on the holdout it costs {{RESULT:fo.extra_capital}} more than P2 for {{RESULT:fo.aog_reduction}} {{RESULT:fo.aog_reduction_ci}} less material AOG a year. That makes it a frontier option for a planner with the budget, not a free lunch.

**6. What should the planner do this week?**
At week {{RESULT:actions.week.as_of}} the Planner Action Centre raises {{RESULT:actions.week.count_total}} actions, {{RESULT:actions.week.count_urgent}} urgent. The first three in the inbox: {{RESULT:actions.top1.summary}}; {{RESULT:actions.top2.summary}}; {{RESULT:actions.top3.summary}}.

